{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the neurology doctors. You came in with dizziness and a headache—can you tell me more about what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I went to bed last night feeling fine, but when I sat up this morning, the room started spinning. I felt like I was on a boat. I had to hold onto furniture to get to the bathroom."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds really unsettling. Did the spinning get better when you moved around?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:05:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little. The spinning lessened, but I still felt off balance. And then I got a headache—started in the back of my head, now it’s up front. It’s achy and constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any nausea with it? Sensitivity to light or sound?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:08:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’ve been nauseous. And light and sound bother me a bit. I’ve also been hearing whispers at night—like someone’s talking, but no one’s there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, thanks. Any weakness, numbness, or trouble speaking?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:11:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No. My arms and legs feel strong. I do have some word-finding trouble sometimes, but that’s been going on since my transplant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. We’re going to do a CT scan of your head to rule out anything serious like a bleed or stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that why I’m here? Because of a possible stroke?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2180-06-17 19:16:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s one possibility, but your symptoms could also be from inner ear issues or even a medication side effect. The scan will help us rule things out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your CT scan came back normal—no bleeding or stroke. That’s good news."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. But what’s causing the dizziness then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not sure yet. Because your dizziness started after a medication change, we’re considering that as a possible cause. You recently started atovaquone, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, about three weeks ago. I was on dapsone before, but they switched me because my white count dropped."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Atovaquone can cause dizziness in some patients. We’re going to order an MRI to get a more detailed look at your brain and blood vessels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that tell us for sure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2180-06-17 20:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’ll help. If the MRI is normal, it makes a medication side effect more likely. We’ll also check your tacrolimus level—it can sometimes cause headaches."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your MRI results are back. No stroke, no bleeding, no blockages in your brain vessels. Everything looks structurally normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no stroke? That’s great. But what is it then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:04:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The pattern of your dizziness—worse with movement, better with stillness, and the unsteadiness—suggests a vestibular issue. Given that it started around the time you began atovaquone, we think the medication is likely contributing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:06:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I’ve had dizziness before. This feels worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and the timing lines up with starting atovaquone. We’ve discussed this with your liver team, and we agree it’s safe to switch you back to dapsone."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But my white count dropped last time. Could that happen again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible, which is why we’ll monitor your blood work closely. You’ll need a CBC in one week to check your white count."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. What about the nausea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve given you ondansetron for that—it should help. We also consulted physical therapy, and they recommend vestibular rehab after discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Vestibular rehab? What’s that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a type of physical therapy that helps your brain adapt to balance changes. It can reduce dizziness over time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d like to try that. And my headache—is it from the dizziness?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Your tacrolimus level was high when you arrived—9.2—but it’s come down to 5.5 today. That may have contributed to the headache, but it’s likely also related to the dizziness and nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So we’re not increasing the dose back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2180-06-18 07:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, your current dose is appropriate. We’ll keep monitoring it at your follow-ups."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "How are you feeling today? Any improvement?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little. The nausea is better with the medication. The dizziness is still there, but not as intense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s good progress. Since you’re stable and improving, we’re planning to discharge you tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What do I need to do after I go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll start dapsone instead of atovaquone. Make sure to get your blood work done in one week to check your white count. Also, schedule vestibular therapy and keep your neurology and hepatology follow-ups."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do that. And if the dizziness gets worse?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2180-06-19 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your doctor. If it’s severe or you develop new symptoms like weakness or confusion, come back to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2180-06-20 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re going to discharge you this morning. Your dizziness has improved, and all tests are reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2180-06-20 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better than when I came in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2180-06-20 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with dapsone, continue your other meds, and follow up as planned. Take it slow with activity, and use a cane if you feel unsteady."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2180-06-20 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. I’m glad we figured it out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2180-06-20 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Me too. We’ll keep working together to manage your symptoms. Safe going home."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2180-06-17 18:46:00",
    "admission_end": "2180-06-20 17:15:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of liver transplant presented with acute worsening of dizziness and a new-onset headache, which began shortly after sitting up one morning. She described vertigo, unsteadiness, nausea, photophobia, and auditory hallucinations of whispering. Initial concern included stroke, but CT and MRI of the brain showed no hemorrhage, infarct, or vascular abnormality. Her symptoms were attributed to a likely adverse effect of atovaquone, which she had started three weeks prior. Her tacrolimus level was initially elevated but normalized during admission. Atovaquone was discontinued and replaced with dapsone, with plans to monitor white blood cell count. She improved with supportive care, including ondansetron for nausea and planned vestibular therapy. She was discharged home in stable condition with follow-up arranged in neurology and hepatology.",
    "problems": [
      "Vertigo of central origin, bilateral",
      "Liver transplant status",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Type 2 diabetes mellitus without complications",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Celiac disease",
      "Vestibular neuronitis, bilateral",
      "Hypothyroidism, unspecified",
      "Decreased white blood cell count, unspecified",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult) (pediatric)",
      "Adverse effect of other specified systemic anti-infectives and antiparasitics, initial encounter",
      "Personal history of nicotine dependence",
      "Long term (current) use of antithrombotics/antiplatelets"
    ]
  }
}
